what's your favorite game one that you  would describe yourself as a fan of now  for me it would have to be Skyrim but do  you love your favorite game enough to  pay several hundred dollars on tickets  accommodation and flights to a country  on the edge of the Arctic Circle to hang  out with other players of the game swap  stories and have a big old party well  you know what you might if your game of  choice is EVE Online thousands of the  spacefaring fans attended fan fest this  year held an Iceland's capital Reykjavik  it's a celebration of all things in the  EVE universe a setting for developers  and players to mix swap stories and just  generally have a good time but what is  going on in our human brains that can  make players of a game so passionate so  loyal so fanatical  now if you haven't heard of EVE Online  it's a massively multiplayer online game  played by over half a million people now  what sets the game apart from other MMOs  is that everyone plays in this same  universe on a single shard so I figured  what better community to use as a case  study to investigate the psychology of  what makes people feel and behave like  fans for me the key to Eve is definitely  the community I mean the people who  always play together and you can hate  your guts one day and just love each  other the next it's an absolutely  awesome feeling and if I let everything  in Eve is seated from the players makes  it even more awesome because everything  is created by us for our game as many a  community and it's really a sandbox  thing that really appeals to me the fact  that whenever you do something you're  messing up someone else's life I think  it's just the community you make friends  and you make enemies you play other  games there's no repercussions for  losing this you lose something and it  burns and you try to lick your wounds  and come back with a little smarter  hopefully and and and move from there  now being a fan of a thing is generally  a positive experience this is  predominately because we get a great  feeling of oneness when we connect to  other people when we self-identify as  part of a group so why is this well a  prominent and established theory of  human psychology and one I've talked  about before on reality check is the  idea of self-determination theory this  gives us an insight into our motivations  why we do things according to the theory  we are all motivated to be competent  autonomous and importantly for us today  and related to other people now this  ties into exactly what those fans were  saying about why they enjoy the games so  much it's all about the community in the  world of social psychology we treat a  group which we belong to as an in-group  one that we self-identify with an  out-group on the other hand is one you  don't belong to now our own innate human  psychology means that we unwittingly tie  our self-esteem end with any group that  we join so not only do we favor  that group and talk it up but we will  actively discriminate against other  groups putting them down in comparison  and an effort to protect our own  self-esteem investment scientific  research has demonstrated this by simply  dividing a number of individuals random  into two groups and then giving them  time to bond people will then actively  favour those members of their own group  and behave derisively towards members of  the other group based on nothing at all  other than the random assignment to that  group in the first place  now I also put the question of what  makes Eve fans so loyal to the game  developers ccp's chief marketing officer  and all-round cheerful chap David read  to see what he thought you literally are  impacting every other player with  everything that you do because everyone  is playing on the same shard together  everyone is in this sandbox style of  design there is a very robust high  fidelity virtual economy under it and  what this means in the end is that as an  Eve player right I get to understand  that this is a game where I really make  a difference right my choices have real  consequences my actions have impact  there is no George Lucas of EVE Online  who is like here's the story here are  the stars here's how it's going to go no  it is all in the hands of the players  this sandbox environment is also crucial  to encouraging a committed fanbase  existing in a world where you can see  that your actions matter and that what  you do makes a difference is really  important in helping to create the  feeling of contingency without this our  illusion of the world can quickly fall  apart and break the relationship we feel  with the game this also fosters a sense  of ownership and fans regularly refer to  Eve as their game this ties into  something called the environment affect  which hypothesizes the people ascribe  more value to things merely because they  own them a lovely little experiment  demonstrated this idea by splitting  participants into two groups one group  were merely shown a mug by a researcher  who kept it in their hands and the other  group were allowed to hold it and told  that they in fact know all the mug both  groups were then asked how much they  would pay for the mug now the second  group who felt they had ownership gave  the mug more than double the  on average compared to the first group  so by giving players control you helped  foster ownership which in turn can lead  to them ascribing it with a higher value  at least that's the theory now if you  talk to anyone who's tried to play Eve  and failed you undoubtedly hear that the  game was just too hard or just too  complicated so I asked David if he  thought that this barrier for entry this  degree of exclusivity might also be  playing a role we are proud to be a  company of adults making core games for  adults who are core gamers and there is  a certain amount of challenge that you  know is part of being a gamer right I  like having I like having bars set and  surpassing them and more and more 